### What are EnumSet and EnumMap?

EnumSet is a specialised Set implementation designed exclusively for enum types. Internally it represents the set as a bit-vector: each enum constant maps to one bit in a `long` value. Adding a constant sets its bit. Removing it clears the bit. Checking membership tests the bit. All operations are single bitwise instructions.

EnumMap is a specialised Map implementation where the key type must be an enum. Internally it stores values in a plain `Object[]` array indexed by `key.ordinal()`. There is no hashing, no bucket lookup, and no collision handling.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.EnumMap;
import java.util.EnumSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class EnumSetEnumMapDemo {
    enum TransactionType { DEBIT, CREDIT, TRANSFER, FEE, INTEREST }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // EnumSet
        Set<TransactionType> allowed = EnumSet.of(
            TransactionType.DEBIT, TransactionType.CREDIT, TransactionType.TRANSFER
        );
        System.out.println("Allowed: " + allowed);
        System.out.println("Contains FEE: " + allowed.contains(TransactionType.FEE));

        // EnumMap
        EnumMap<TransactionType, Integer> dailyCounts = new EnumMap<>(TransactionType.class);
        dailyCounts.put(TransactionType.DEBIT, 1520);
        dailyCounts.put(TransactionType.CREDIT, 890);
        dailyCounts.put(TransactionType.TRANSFER, 340);
        dailyCounts.put(TransactionType.FEE, 45);
        dailyCounts.put(TransactionType.INTEREST, 12);

        System.out.println("Daily counts: " + dailyCounts);
        System.out.println("Debit count: " + dailyCounts.get(TransactionType.DEBIT));
    }
}
```

```text
Allowed: [DEBIT, CREDIT, TRANSFER]
Contains FEE: false
Daily counts: {DEBIT=1520, CREDIT=890, TRANSFER=340, FEE=45, INTEREST=12}
Debit count: 1520
```

### How does EnumSet work internally?

EnumSet has two internal implementations chosen automatically based on the number of constants in the enum.

- **RegularEnumSet** (up to 64 constants) stores the entire set in a single `long`. Each constant's ordinal maps to one bit.
- **JumboEnumSet** (more than 64 constants) uses a `long[]` array.

Operations map directly to bitwise instructions.

| Operation | Bitwise instruction | Time complexity |
|---|---|---|
| add(e) | elements \|= (1L << e.ordinal()) | O(1) |
| remove(e) | elements &= ~(1L << e.ordinal()) | O(1) |
| contains(e) | (elements & (1L << e.ordinal())) != 0 | O(1) |
| size() | Long.bitCount(elements) | O(1) |
| isEmpty() | elements == 0 | O(1) |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.EnumSet;

public class EnumSetInternalsDemo {
    enum AccountStatus { ACTIVE, FROZEN, CLOSED, DORMANT, UNDER_REVIEW }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EnumSet<AccountStatus> statuses = EnumSet.noneOf(AccountStatus.class);
        // Internal long = 0b00000 (all bits off)

        statuses.add(AccountStatus.ACTIVE);       // 0b00001
        statuses.add(AccountStatus.UNDER_REVIEW); // 0b10001

        System.out.println("Set: " + statuses);
        System.out.println("Contains FROZEN: " + statuses.contains(AccountStatus.FROZEN));
        System.out.println("Size: " + statuses.size());

        // Factory methods
        EnumSet<AccountStatus> all = EnumSet.allOf(AccountStatus.class);
        EnumSet<AccountStatus> complement = EnumSet.complementOf(statuses);
        EnumSet<AccountStatus> range = EnumSet.range(AccountStatus.ACTIVE, AccountStatus.CLOSED);

        System.out.println("All: " + all);
        System.out.println("Complement: " + complement);
        System.out.println("Range ACTIVE..CLOSED: " + range);
    }
}
```

```text
Set: [ACTIVE, UNDER_REVIEW]
Contains FROZEN: false
Size: 2
All: [ACTIVE, FROZEN, CLOSED, DORMANT, UNDER_REVIEW]
Complement: [FROZEN, CLOSED, DORMANT]
Range ACTIVE..CLOSED: [ACTIVE, FROZEN, CLOSED]
```

### How does EnumMap work internally?

EnumMap maintains an `Object[] vals` array of size equal to the number of enum constants. The index into this array is `key.ordinal()`. There is no hashing and no collision handling.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.EnumMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class EnumMapInternalsDemo {
    enum LoanType { HOME, PERSONAL, EDUCATION, VEHICLE, GOLD }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        EnumMap<LoanType, BigDecimal> interestRates = new EnumMap<>(LoanType.class);
        interestRates.put(LoanType.HOME, new BigDecimal("8.50"));
        interestRates.put(LoanType.PERSONAL, new BigDecimal("12.75"));
        interestRates.put(LoanType.EDUCATION, new BigDecimal("7.25"));
        interestRates.put(LoanType.VEHICLE, new BigDecimal("9.50"));
        interestRates.put(LoanType.GOLD, new BigDecimal("10.00"));

        // Direct array access: vals[LoanType.HOME.ordinal()] = 8.50
        System.out.println("Home loan rate: " + interestRates.get(LoanType.HOME) + "%");

        // Iteration follows declaration order
        for (Map.Entry<LoanType, BigDecimal> entry : interestRates.entrySet()) {
            System.out.println(entry.getKey() + ": " + entry.getValue() + "%");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Home loan rate: 8.50%
HOME: 8.50%
PERSONAL: 12.75%
EDUCATION: 7.25%
VEHICLE: 9.50%
GOLD: 10.00%
```

### How are EnumSet and EnumMap different from HashSet and HashMap?

| Feature | EnumSet | HashSet | EnumMap | HashMap |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Backing structure | Bit-vector (long) | HashMap internally | Object[] array | Node[] with chains/trees |
| Hash computation | None (ordinal = bit position) | hashCode() | None (ordinal = index) | hashCode() + spread |
| Memory (5 entries) | 8 bytes (one long) | ~240 bytes (5 Nodes) | ~40 bytes (5 array slots) | ~400 bytes (5 Nodes + array) |
| contains/get speed | ~2 ns (bitwise AND) | ~20 ns (hash + equals) | ~2 ns (array access) | ~20 ns (hash + equals) |
| Iteration order | Enum declaration order | Unpredictable | Enum declaration order | Unpredictable |
| Null values | Not allowed | Allowed | Allowed | Allowed |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;

public class EnumVsHashPerformanceDemo {
    enum Permission { READ, WRITE, DELETE, ADMIN, AUDIT }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // EnumSet: compact, fast
        Set<Permission> enumPerms = EnumSet.of(Permission.READ, Permission.WRITE);

        // HashSet: heavier
        Set<Permission> hashPerms = new HashSet<>(List.of(Permission.READ, Permission.WRITE));

        // Both behave the same functionally
        System.out.println("EnumSet contains READ: " + enumPerms.contains(Permission.READ));
        System.out.println("HashSet contains READ: " + hashPerms.contains(Permission.READ));

        // But EnumSet is far more efficient internally
        System.out.println("EnumSet class: " + enumPerms.getClass().getSimpleName());
    }
}
```

```text
EnumSet contains READ: true
HashSet contains READ: true
EnumSet class: RegularEnumSet
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Using HashSet or HashMap for enum keys

```java
// WRONG: HashSet for enums is wasteful
Set<Permission> perms = new HashSet<>();
perms.add(Permission.READ);
perms.add(Permission.WRITE);
// Works, but wastes memory and CPU on hashing
```

```java
// CORRECT: EnumSet is purpose-built for enums
Set<Permission> perms = EnumSet.of(Permission.READ, Permission.WRITE);
// Single long, bitwise operations, 30x less memory
```

#### Mistake: Trying to create EnumSet with the constructor

```java
// WRONG: EnumSet has no public constructor
// EnumSet<Permission> perms = new EnumSet<>(); // COMPILE ERROR
```

```java
// CORRECT: Use factory methods
EnumSet<Permission> none = EnumSet.noneOf(Permission.class);
EnumSet<Permission> all = EnumSet.allOf(Permission.class);
EnumSet<Permission> some = EnumSet.of(Permission.READ, Permission.WRITE);
```

### What are the edge cases?

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.EnumMap;
import java.util.EnumSet;

public class EnumEdgeCasesDemo {
    enum Empty {} // zero constants
    enum Single { ONLY }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Edge case 1: EnumSet with single-constant enum
        EnumSet<Single> single = EnumSet.allOf(Single.class);
        System.out.println("Single-constant set: " + single);

        // Edge case 2: EnumMap allows null values but not null keys
        EnumMap<Single, String> map = new EnumMap<>(Single.class);
        map.put(Single.ONLY, null);
        System.out.println("Null value in EnumMap: " + map.get(Single.ONLY));

        // Edge case 3: EnumSet.noneOf still needs the enum class
        EnumSet<Single> empty = EnumSet.noneOf(Single.class);
        System.out.println("Empty EnumSet size: " + empty.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Single-constant set: [ONLY]
Null value in EnumMap: null
Empty EnumSet size: 0
```

### What about thread safety?

EnumSet and EnumMap are not synchronised. For concurrent access, wrap them with `Collections.synchronizedSet()` or `Collections.synchronizedMap()`. Alternatively, use `ConcurrentHashMap` with enum keys if you need concurrent read-write access, though you lose the memory and speed benefits.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.EnumMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.EnumSet;

public class SynchronizedEnumDemo {
    enum AccountType { SAVINGS, CURRENT, FIXED_DEPOSIT }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set<AccountType> syncSet = Collections.synchronizedSet(
            EnumSet.of(AccountType.SAVINGS, AccountType.CURRENT)
        );
        Map<AccountType, String> syncMap = Collections.synchronizedMap(
            new EnumMap<>(AccountType.class)
        );
        syncMap.put(AccountType.SAVINGS, "Rajesh");
        System.out.println("Synchronized EnumMap: " + syncMap);
    }
}
```

```text
Synchronized EnumMap: {SAVINGS=Rajesh}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Bit manipulation.** EnumSet operations compile to single CPU instructions: OR for add, AND-NOT for remove, AND for contains. This is as fast as it gets on modern hardware.
- **EnumSet.allOf vs EnumSet.noneOf.** For permission checks, start with noneOf and add specific permissions (whitelist) rather than allOf and remove (blacklist).
- **Serialization.** EnumSet serializes by storing the enum class and the bit-vector. Adding new enum constants at the end is safe. Reordering constants changes ordinals and breaks serialized EnumSets.
- **EnumMap with streams.** Use `Collectors.groupingBy()` with an EnumMap supplier for grouping:

```java
import java.util.EnumMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

// Group transactions by type into an EnumMap
// transactions.stream().collect(
//     Collectors.groupingBy(Transaction::getType,
//         () -> new EnumMap<>(TransactionType.class),
//         Collectors.toList())
// );
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why is EnumSet faster than HashSet?" : EnumSet uses a bit-vector where each operation is a single bitwise instruction. No hashing, no equals comparison, no object allocation.
- "Can EnumSet hold null?" : No. EnumSet does not permit null elements and throws NullPointerException.
- "What happens if the enum has more than 64 constants?" : EnumSet uses JumboEnumSet backed by a long[] array.
- "Does EnumMap maintain insertion order?" : No, it maintains enum declaration order (ordinal order).
